this is a production of cornell  university  yep i'm in uh rebecca nelson's lab and  today i'm going to be talking to you all  about  exploring the genetic basis of fusarium  ear rot resistance  and field component traits so i just  want to  direct your attention to this nasty  looking ear on the left  and these white streaks that are on the  on the kernels  and those are signs of fusarium ear rot  this is a disease caused by a fungal  pathogen fusarium verticillioids  it can infect the ear by a few different  methods  either by systemic affection or more  commonly  via the silk channels or injury to the  ear most commonly  due to insects and if you serum ear rot  we saw those sort of streaked  starburst symptoms that are signs of the  fungus  on the kernels and  it can have a yield penalty just by  you know being an ear rot but more  concerningly  is that the fungus also produces these  fumonos and mycotoxins  now these mycotoxins affect human and  livestock health  they're regulated uh in the united  states uh this is the range for human  consumption  and this uh females in b1 is the most  toxic form  it sort of resembles uh lipid and  affects lipid synthesis  um and so there's uh because of this  regulation there can be an economic cost  if a grower has  corn that's over these limits now these  are  most commonly found in uh tropical and  subtropical climates  uh much warmer than we have around here  um  so most of the work i'll be talking  about today or all of the work actually  was performed in north carolina and it  is a significant disease in  southern united states corn production  however i think maybe within my lifetime  we'll be doing  some fusion eret trials here in new york  as well  now this disease has been kind of worked  on in our lab for about the past 10  years  and in particular a past grad student  laura morales was really interested in  looking at  how this disease related to different  ear traits or  yield component traits so  to kind of set the scene laura had  looked at  within the goodman diversity panel  and found that in the temperate  subpopulation these yield component  traits shown here clustered here  uh did not correlate with fusion ear rot  however if you analyze the tropical  subpopulation  they're actually positively correlated  indicating that perhaps if you're using  tropical germplasm  breeding for increased yield might also  increase susceptibility to ear rot  and vice versa if you breed for uh  resistance you might  uh affect yield so that's  a little concerning and uh laura looked  deeper into this  in a 2019 paper where she  used nam families to identify some  qtl and this is a busy table but i just  want to highlight  those in blue or qtl that  she found affected both fusarium ear  erot resistance  as well as ear traits while those here  in orangish  are those that were specific to fusarium  ear rot resistance  so she found many qtl and i've just  highlighted a few here that i'll be  talking about more later  so this was sort of the platform for me  to uh  take off from some of her work and try  to really have the goal of  better understanding how fusarium era  and these yield component traits are  controlled within loci  um are there is plyo trophy occurring  or is it all linkage of different genes  um  and also do we have this sort of only  have this  uh serum era ear trade trade-off  or can you have sort of a double win  scenario  and then are there different dynamics  going on in tropical  versus temperate germplasm so  really the basis of my work has been  using this nested  near isogenic line population it's  developed about like 15 years ago or so  by syngenta and you can now acquire seed  from a cement  and what's nice is that this nil  population  was developed using b73 as their current  parent  and 18 of the namm founders as the donor  parents  we acquired our lab acquired these nils  in two separate sets  and so they've been those two sets have  been analyzed by  uh judy kolkman for northern leaf flight  and she found  some nice uh results for uh  loci that are affecting mlb resistance  and laura also has a paper looking at  the separate set  for southern leaf blight resistance so  both foliar diseases  but we've now been able to sort of unify  all of these together  and what i'll be talking about today  will be the first time we've been using  it on  an ear rot disease  so to give you a sense of what this sort  of mill germplasm looks like across the  genome  i'm showing here the introgression  tiling path  for about 10 of them that  are included in the experiment i'm going  to be talking about today  here on the y-axis each tick mark is  just a different nil  um couldn't include the names uh because  if there's so many  and then the blue lines are the donor  introductions which  within each of those nils and so all the  white space is just representing your  b73 recurrent parent and so you can see  that even  with just 10 percent of our total mil  population we can get pretty good  coverage of the entire  genome  now i wanted to build off of laura's  work and so  um for my experiment i selected mills  that were  had introductions within the qtl that  she had previously identified  that i showed earlier in that table here  the blue  uh bluish uh highlight represents those  trade off  qtl that have affect both fer  and different ear traits whereas these  orangish bases qtl  are those that affect just fer  now to zoom on zoom in on just one of  those  uh we can still see that this chromosome  one  qtl here that laura identified  sort of in orange here has nine  nils with introductions that are  contained within it  um and what's  where you're trying to see is whether  these nils actually contain  the variation that she had observed and  can we  sort of uh validate uh her qtl effect as  well as try to  um we have more donors  in this population and try to see if we  can understand more about  the genetic control of that locus  so our experimental design was to grow  all of our nils  out here in beautiful clayton north  carolina  um inoculate them with serious silio  ladies  and as i mentioned earlier there were  196 mills in this experiment along with  their founder parents and not all of  those were  included in were from my experiment per  se  also miriam maoun a former postdoc in  our lab and our lab manager isrepka also  had mills  in this experiment so some of the  results i'm showing you are  representing uh mills that they had  included in their experiments as well  but they were all grown together under  the same conditions and same treatment  in four plots inoculated plots and three  ears were harvested per plot for  phenotyping  so for phenotyping we just took some  visual estimates  for disease and insect damage  and we also utilized this flatbed  scanner pipeline  developed by nathan miller at the  university of wisconsin  and so you can see an image from that  here you get nice cob length and  cob width measurements as well as you  can get mesh metrics of cob color  and we took some other yield component  traits and we're actually still working  through some of this stuff to get  their mycotoxin data as well as to get  scanned kernel traits  now i've taken a couple different  approaches to try and analyze this data  one is a pretty basic one to just look  at within a qtl  so for example within this chromosome  one qtl i showed you before  seeing whether we can just fit an anova  model to see whether  there's variation among those nils  for that trait another is to take a more  genome-wide  approach and using all of our phenotype  run association mapping uh for  each of the traits  so slightly uh discouragingly uh  this within qtl analysis using anovas to  look for  variation within each of the traits that  laura had previously found  did not find any significant  differences so that was slightly  discouraging  but looking at genome-wide also for  fusion  era it was slightly frustrating to not  find significant  results for this however  when looking at some of our yield  component traits  uh we did find significant hits for cob  length on our geos  here a couple on chromosome one as well  as chromosomes eight and ten  and i particularly want to note here on  uh  chromosome 1 around 50 megabase pairs  we also found a hit for this cobb red  intensity tree and  this cobb color is a trait that's  actually  has some major effect loci uh unlike  um sort of fusarium era which is all  quantitative  um as well as many of the  sort of gear traits cob length for  example  and so here we can see we have some nice  peaks uh  that indicate we have some variation in  our population controlling  sort of some of these contrasts that i'm  showing up here  in cobb color and when you  look at some major effect loci you can  see that  we can identify  some co-localization between many of  these cloned  major effect genes and our  guess peaks here and i particularly want  to highlight pericarp  color 1 which is  previously been shown to be involved in  fumonosyn resistance  so i found it interesting that uh  this region seems to be although we  didn't have variation in fusion erot  we do have uh a hit for it  possibly a gene that's involved in uh  humanitarian resistance as well  so to summarize uh our scanner  phenotyping and that pipeline  along with their near isogenic lines  provide a rich data set to explore these  sorts of issues sort of our preliminary  within qtl analysis hasn't yielded much  but association mapping did find some  significant loci  that are promising and i'm interested in  following up on  and hopefully more to come as  phenotyping  and analysis progresses i want to  finish by thanking everyone in the  nelson lab especially  those bolded who contributed a lot to  this work  as well as our collaborators at north  carolina state  who helped us with field space and  inoculations  funding sources and of course thanks to  all the  beautiful corn that had to suffer for  this experiment  so thanks for listening and i'm happy to  take questions  sure um that's a clap too um  good job will um so it looks like your  nil 1194 has a relatively narrow region  when you tile it with the other  nils in your qtl region do you have an  idea of how many  genes are in that narrow region that you  could look for candidates  um i don't and  i guess there's also the concern that it  might not  contain the you know i i don't know  where in that qtl  the genes controlling these uh  phenotypes  are so it could be actually not even in  that intragression  at all um but  yeah not sure how many um genes are in  there  and not even sure if the genes were  interested or even contained in that  introduction  so to follow up with that would it be  worth you know what the nam parent is  for that  in aggression line so would it be worth  going to the nam rails  and just inoculating them and seeing if  you could replicate that  that qtl region by mapping  yeah so this was um that's how that's  laura had used the nam rails to  initially identify these qtl so that's  sort of what we're  working off of  i guess that's sort of already been done  not not in all of the families though i  will say that  so we we're including more  donor parents in our our study  this has been a production of cornell  university  on the web at cornell.edu

